## The subject

Read these files first:
- /root/DefiElements/viz/src/data.ts       (59 elements, 29 laws, 19 hazard rules)
- /root/DefiElements/viz/src/protocols.ts  (12 real protocols as element subsets, 4 dead)
- /root/DefiElements/viz/src/laws.ts       (a parser + closure engine I already wrote in TypeScript)
- /root/DefiElements/research/QUESTIONS.md (the open questions - especially 11-14)

## What the data is

An element is a recurring financial mechanism. A protocol is a SET of elements.
The 29 laws are constraints of the form:
  (subject1 | subject2 | ...) -> term1 + term2 + ...
where each term is a disjunction of elements. The law fires if any subject is
present; it is satisfied if every term has at least one of its alternatives
present. 19 hazard rules name element combinations that are forbidden,
elevated-risk, or unverifiable.

## Your task

Build a Quint specification in /root/DefiElements/formal/ that models this.
Concretely:

1. `atlas.qnt` - the vocabulary: elements, their attributes, the laws as a
   relation, the hazard rules.
2. A state machine where a protocol is ASSEMBLED incrementally: the state is
   the set of elements chosen so far, and an action adds one element.
3. Express as invariants/properties:
   - `closure`: every fired law is satisfied
   - `hazardFree`: no hazard rule has all its elements present
   - whatever temporal property best captures the reflexive loop (question 13)
4. RUN the model checker. `quint verify` or `quint run --invariant=...`.

## What I actually want out of this

Findings, not just a spec. Specifically:

- **Does model checking find hazard combinations that are NOT in the 19 rules?**
  Reachable element sets that violate a safety property but nobody wrote down.
  That is question 12 and it is the most valuable possible result.
- Do the 12 real protocols satisfy `closure`? My TypeScript engine says Aave,
  Maker, Liquity, GMX and CoW do; Lido, CCTP, Centrifuge, Terra, Mango and
  Euler do NOT. Confirm or refute independently.
- Is stratum S0..S4 derivable as topological rank over the law graph, and where
  does it disagree with the hand-assigned depth? (question 10)
- Can the `|` alternatives be treated as nondeterministic choice, and does that
  produce a meaningful lattice of legal protocols? (question 14)

## Constraints

- Do NOT modify anything outside /root/DefiElements/formal/.
- If quint is not installed, install it (npm i -g @informalsystems/quint) or
  report clearly that you could not.
- If the full model is too large to check, SAY SO and check a reduced instance
  (e.g. one family group, or a bounded number of elements) rather than
  pretending. Report the bound you used.
- Report negative results honestly. "The model checker found nothing new" is a
  real and useful finding.

## Output

Write your findings to /root/DefiElements/formal/FINDINGS.md - what you modelled,
what you ran, what it found, what it could not do, and specifically what any of
it implies for VISUALISING this concept. End with the single most surprising
thing you learned.

